      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155460,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1162,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1124,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1163,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155461,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1163,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours arise in the deep soft tissue, with occasional exceptions {26504053}, most often in the lower extremity but also in the chest/abdominal wall, upper extremity, abdomen/retroperitoneum, and head and neck {26504053; 11406662; 8333559}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1125,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155462,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1164,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients present with rapidly growing, usually deep, variably painful soft tissue swellings {11406662}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1126,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155463,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1165,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are rare and are most common in the sixth to seventh decades of life (mean age: ~72&nbsp;years), occurring more frequently in men, with an M:F&nbsp;ratio of 1.8:1 {26504053}. Similar-appearing tumours in paediatric age groups are considered to represent embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1127,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155464,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1166,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1128,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155465,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1167,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcomas have complex karyotypes with numerical and unbalanced structural changes, with frequent <em>TP53</em> mutations, <em>RB1</em> alterations, and <em>CDKN2A</em> deletions {19480930}. Genome-wide surveys have identified recurrent losses of DNA (e.g. 10q23, 6q), gains (e.g. 1p23-p22, 17p, and 11q), and amplifications {12550764; 38722224; 37593416}. The genetic profiles of pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcomas mirror those of undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcomas {30709449; 38722224; 37593416}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1135,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1174,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155466,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1174,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcomas are usually large, well-marginated tumours {26504053}, with pseudocapsules and whitish or fleshy cut surfaces, often with necrosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1129,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155467,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1168,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcomas have a variable appearance with markedly atypical spindled, epithelioid, round, and/or pleomorphic cells and a variable component (sometimes focal) of multinucleated, polygonal, spindled, or rhabdoid cells with eosinophilic cytoplasm. Tumours typically display desmin expression of varying extent (sometimes diffuse), with MYOD1 and myogenin expression (sometimes focal). Some tumours showing the morphological features of pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcoma represent other tumour types with divergent rhabdomyoblastic differentiation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1130,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155468,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1169,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Aspirated material shows high cellularity, with large pleomorphic, epithelioid, spindle, and rhabdoid cells characterized by abundant eccentric basophilic cytoplasm and pleomorphic nuclei, in tissue fragments and as single cells, with lymphocytes and binucleated to multinucleated giant cells {32159876}. Background necrosis and atypical mitoses are present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1131,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1170,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155469,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1170,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1134,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1173,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155470,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1173,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> high-grade sarcoma with pleomorphic, spindle, epithelioid, and/or round cells and a variable number of cells with brightly eosinophilic cytoplasm and variable expression of desmin and myogenin or MYOD1.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1132,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1171,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155471,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1171,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This tumour can be staged using the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) or American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) staging for soft tissue sarcomas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1133,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1172,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 155472,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 1172,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These highly aggressive sarcomas were associated with a median survival of 7.3&nbsp;months in the largest study to date; about 80% of patients died of disease {26504053}. Metastases to the lungs are common {26504053; 11406662; 8333559}. s</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T16:12:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]